`lib/App/karr/BoardStore.pm:342-350`. `restore_snapshot` calls
`delete_all_karr_refs` **first** and then writes the refs back one by one.
`write_ref` is unguarded and there is no rollback, so any write that fails
leaves the board partially — or, if it fails early, completely — destroyed.
`Cmd/Restore.pm` validates the snapshot's version and shape but never the ref
names it contains.

Reproduced with a genuine `karr backup` snapshot plus one extra key
`refs/karr/bad name` (which sorts first):

    karr restore --yes --input bad.yml
    -> libgit2 error -12 (klass 4): the given reference name
       'refs/karr/bad name' is not valid    EXIT=1
    local  AFTER: 0 refs   (was 5)
    origin AFTER: 0 refs   (was 5)

The remote wipe is the END-block push insurance from #37 doing its job — it
faithfully mirrors the half-executed destruction, `--prune` and all. The user
gets one cryptic libgit2 line (with no trailing newline, so it runs into the
next output) and no indication that the board is gone, locally and remotely.

The disaster-recovery tool is the thing that causes the disaster. Fix: validate
every ref name in the snapshot **before** deleting anything, and write into the
live board only after all writes can be known to succeed — or stage the new
refs under a temporary namespace and swap, so a failure leaves the old board
intact.
Fixed and merged into main; full suite green at 88 files / 566 tests.

Every one of these was reproduced before it was fixed and left a regression test
that was verified to fail against the pre-fix code -- not a test that merely
passes now. Details and what was deliberately NOT fixed are in the merge commit
that names this ticket.
